        // 私有构造函数
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, if a class has only a private constructor, then that class cannot be instantiated. In such cases, we usually expect it to be a final class to prevent inheritance, as inheritance does not make sense.

##### Rule Meaning
Declaring a class that contains only private constructors as a final class clearly indicates that the class should not be inherited, enhancing code maintainability and readability.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public class UtilityClass {
    private UtilityClass() {
        // Private constructor
    }
}
```

