#### 规则概述
方法的参数列表如果包含过多的参数，会使代码的可读性和可维护性变差，增加理解和使用该方法的难度。

##### 规则意义
保持方法参数的数量合理，有助于提升代码的可读性和可维护性。过多的参数容易导致代码错误，难以追踪和修复问题。此外，较多的参数可能会让方法调用变得复杂且难以理解。

##### 错误示例
```java
public void processOrder(String orderId, String customerName, String shippingAddress, String productCode, int quantity, String paymentMethod, String discountCode, boolean isGift, String giftMessage) {
    // 处理订单的逻辑
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public void processOrder(Order order) {
    // 处理订单的逻辑
}

class Order {
    private String orderId;
    private String customerName;
    private String shippingAddress;
    private String productCode;
    private int quantity;
    private String paymentMethod;
    private String discountCode;
    private boolean isGift;
    private String giftMessage;
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
Having an excessive number of parameters in a method's parameter list can reduce the readability and maintainability of the code, increasing the difficulty of understanding and using that method.

##### Significance of the Rule
Maintaining a reasonable number of method parameters helps improve the readability and maintainability of the code. Too many parameters can lead to errors in the code, making it hard to trace and fix issues. Additionally, having numerous parameters may complicate method calls and make them difficult to understand.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public void processOrder(String orderId, String customerName, String shippingAddress, String productCode, int quantity, String paymentMethod, String discountCode, boolean isGift, String giftMessage) {
    // 处理订单的逻辑
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public void processOrder(Order order) {
    // 处理订单的逻辑
}

class Order {
    private String orderId;
    private String customerName;
    private String shippingAddress;
    private String productCode;
    private int quantity;
    private String paymentMethod;
    private String discountCode;
    private boolean isGift;
    private String giftMessage;
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则用于检查一个类中公共方法的数量是否过多。

##### 规则意义
公共方法的数量过多可能会导致类的复杂性增加，降低代码的可读性和可维护性。过于复杂的类通常不易于测试和扩展，影响了系统的整体设计。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class ExampleClass {
    public void method1() {}
    public void method2() {}
    public void method3() {}
    public void method4() {}
    public void method5() {}
    public void method6() {}
    public void method7() {}
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class ExampleClass {
    private void method1() {}
    private void method2() {}

    public void publicMethod() {
        method1();
        method2();
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule is used to check whether the number of public methods in a class is excessive.

##### Rule Significance
An excessive number of public methods can lead to increased complexity of the class, reducing code readability and maintainability. Classes that are too complex are often difficult to test and extend, impacting the overall design of the system.

##### Error Example
```java
public class ExampleClass {
    public void method1() {}
    public void method2() {}
    public void method3() {}
    public void method4() {}
    public void method5() {}
    public void method6() {}
    public void method7() {}
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class ExampleClass {
    private void method1() {}
    private void method2() {}

    public void publicMethod() {
        method1();
        method2();
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
该规则要求在使用switch语句时，必须包含一个default分支。这样可以确保即使在没有匹配任何case的情况下，程序仍然能够正常运行。

##### 规则意义
使用switch语句而不提供default分支可能导致在处理未考虑的情况时，程序产生不可预测的行为，从而影响代码的稳定性和可维护性。为此，建议始终为每个switch语句提供default分支，以提高代码的健壮性。

##### 错误示例
```java
switch (value) {
    case 1:
        // 处理情况1
        break;
    case 2:
        // 处理情况2
        break;
    // 没有default分支，可能导致未处理的情况
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
switch (value) {
    case 1:
        // 处理情况1
        break;
    case 2:
        // 处理情况2
        break;
    default:
        // 处理未匹配的情况
        break;
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule requires that a default branch be included when using a switch statement. This ensures that the program can operate normally even when no case matches.

##### Rule Significance
Using a switch statement without providing a default branch can lead to unpredictable behavior when handling unconsidered situations, affecting the stability and maintainability of the code. Therefore, it is recommended to always provide a default branch for each switch statement to enhance the robustness of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
switch (value) {
    case 1:
        // Handle case 1
        break;
    case 2:
        // Handle case 2
        break;
    // No default branch, which may lead to unhandled situations
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
switch (value) {
    case 1:
        // Handle case 1
        break;
    case 2:
        // Handle case 2
        break;
    default:
        // Handle unmatched cases
        break;
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在Java中，所有类都隐式地继承自`Object`类。因此，显式地继承`Object`并没有实际意义，可能导致代码可读性降低。

##### 规则意义
建议避免显式地继承`Object`，以提高代码的清晰度并遵循Java语言的最佳实践。

##### 错误示例
```java
class MyClass extends Object {
    // 这里是错误的做法，`MyClass` 自动继承自 `Object`
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
class MyClass {
    // 正确的方式，自动继承自 Object，代码更简洁
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, all classes implicitly inherit from the `Object` class. Therefore, explicitly inheriting from `Object` has no real significance and may lead to reduced code readability.

##### Rule Meaning
It is recommended to avoid explicitly inheriting from `Object` to enhance code clarity and adhere to best practices in Java.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
class MyClass extends Object {
    // This is an incorrect approach; `MyClass` automatically inherits from `Object`
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
class MyClass {
    // Correct approach, automatically inherits from Object, making the code cleaner
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在类的定义中，所有字段的声明应放在类的开始部分。这有助于提高代码的可读性和可维护性。

##### 规则意义
将字段声明集中放置于类的顶部可以使代码结构更加清晰。这样做不仅能够更容易地跟踪和理解类的状态，还可以帮助开发者在查看类时迅速获取其成员变量的信息。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class Example {
    public void someMethod() {
        int x;
    }

    private int myField;
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class Example {
    private int myField;

    public void someMethod() {
        int x;
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In class definitions, all field declarations should be placed at the beginning of the class. This helps improve the readability and maintainability of the code.

##### Significance of the Rule
Concentrating field declarations at the top of the class can make the code structure clearer. This approach not only makes it easier to track and understand the class's state but also helps developers quickly obtain information about its member variables when viewing the class.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public class Example {
    public void someMethod() {
        int x;
    }

    private int myField;
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class Example {
    private int myField;

    public void someMethod() {
        int x;
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
该规则指出，不必要的最终字段可以被声明为静态字段，从而提高内存效率和优化性能。

##### 规则意义
如果一个字段被声明为`final`且不依赖于实例状态，则将其改为`static`可以节省内存，因为静态字段在类加载时被分配，而不必为每个实例分配一个新的副本。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class Example {
    private final String MESSAGE = "Hello, World!";
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class Example {
    private static final String MESSAGE = "Hello, World!";
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule states that unnecessary final fields can be declared as static fields, thereby improving memory efficiency and optimizing performance.

##### Rule Meaning
If a field is declared as `final` and does not depend on instance state, changing it to `static` can save memory, as static fields are allocated when the class is loaded, avoiding the need to allocate a new copy for each instance.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public class Example {
    private final String MESSAGE = "Hello, World!";
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class Example {
    private static final String MESSAGE = "Hello, World!";
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在抽象方法中声明形参为`final`是没有意义的，因为这些参数在子类中会被重写，在重写时无法保证其为`final`，这可能导致不必要的混淆。

##### 规则意义
使用`final`修饰符可以防止参数在方法内被修改，但在抽象方法中，参数的实际实现将在子类中定义。因此，在抽象方法中使用`final`修饰参数是不合适的。

##### 错误示例
```java
public abstract class AbstractClass {
    public abstract void method(final int param);
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public abstract class AbstractClass {
    public abstract void method(int param);
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
Declaring parameters as `final` in an abstract method is meaningless because these parameters will be overridden in subclasses, and there's no guarantee that they will remain `final` during the override process, which may lead to unnecessary confusion.

##### Rule Significance
The `final` modifier can prevent parameters from being modified within the method, but in abstract methods, the actual implementation of the parameters will be defined in subclasses. Therefore, it is inappropriate to use the `final` modifier on parameters in abstract methods.

##### Error Example
```java
public abstract class AbstractClass {
    public abstract void method(final int param);
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public abstract class AbstractClass {
    public abstract void method(int param);
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则检查类的 `finalize` 方法是否调用了超类的 `finalize` 方法。如果未调用，可能导致资源未被正确释放。

##### 规则意义
在Java中，当一个对象被垃圾回收时，系统会调用其 `finalize` 方法进行资源的清理。如果一个类的 `finalize` 方法没有调用超类的 `finalize`，可能会导致父类对象的资源未正确释放，从而造成内存泄漏或其他资源管理问题。

##### 错误示例
```java
class MyClass extends SomeBaseClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 没有调用 super.finalize()
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
class MyClass extends SomeBaseClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 清理自身资源
        // 调用超类的 finalize 方法
        super.finalize();
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule checks whether the `finalize` method of a class calls the `finalize` method of its superclass. Failing to do so may lead to resources not being properly released.

##### Rule Meaning
In Java, when an object is garbage collected, the system calls its `finalize` method to clean up resources. If a class's `finalize` method does not call the superclass's `finalize`, it may result in the resources of the parent class object not being properly released, potentially causing memory leaks or other resource management issues.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
class MyClass extends SomeBaseClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Missing call to super.finalize()
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
class MyClass extends SomeBaseClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Clean up own resources
        // Call superclass's finalize method
        super.finalize();
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则旨在确保在自定义的`finalize`方法中只调用超类的`finalize`方法，而不执行其他逻辑。

##### 规则意义
如果在`finalize`方法中除了调用超类的`finalize`方法外还执行其他的逻辑，可能会导致资源释放不当或不必要的复杂性，增加潜在的内存泄漏风险。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 错误示例：除了调用super.finalize()外还执行其他逻辑
        // 执行某些清理操作
        cleanUpResources();
        super.finalize();
    }
    private void cleanUpResources() {
        // 资源清理逻辑
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 正确示例：仅调用超类finalize方法
        super.finalize();
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule aims to ensure that only the superclass's `finalize` method is called in the custom `finalize` method, without executing any other logic.

##### Significance of the Rule
If additional logic is executed in the `finalize` method aside from calling the superclass's `finalize` method, it may lead to improper resource release or unnecessary complexity, increasing the potential risk of memory leaks.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Incorrect example: executing other logic in addition to calling super.finalize()
        // Performing some cleanup operations
        cleanUpResources();
        super.finalize();
    }
    private void cleanUpResources() {
        // Resource cleanup logic
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Correct example: only calling the superclass finalize method
        super.finalize();
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在 Java 中，`finalize()` 方法用于清理对象资源。重载这个方法可能导致难以理解的行为，造成内存泄露或难以预测的结果。因此，不应该重载 finalize 方法。

##### 规则意义
重载 `finalize()` 方法可能导致潜在的资源管理问题，尤其是在复杂的对象引用中。遵循这一规则可以提高代码的可维护性和可理解性。

##### 错误示例
'''java
public class Example {
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 资源清理代码
    }
    protected void finalize(int code) throws Throwable {
        // 另一种资源清理代码
    }
}
'''

##### 正确示例
'''java
public class Example {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 仅实现一次 finalize 方法
    }
}
'''

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, the `finalize()` method is used for cleaning up object resources. Overriding this method may lead to confusing behavior, causing memory leaks or unpredictable results. Therefore, the `finalize` method should not be overridden.

##### Rule Significance
Overriding the `finalize()` method may result in potential resource management issues, especially in complex object references. Adhering to this rule can improve code maintainability and understandability.

##### Incorrect Example
'''java
public class Example {
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Resource cleanup code
    }
    protected void finalize(int code) throws Throwable {
        // Another type of resource cleanup code
    }
}
'''

##### Correct Example
'''java
public class Example {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Implement finalize method only once
    }
}
'''

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在Java中，`finalize`方法应被定义为`protected`，而不是`public`或`private`。这能确保只有该类及其子类能够调用它。

##### 规则意义
将`finalize`方法设为`protected`可以防止其他不相关的类访问它，从而减少潜在的错误和滥用。这符合封装原则，提升了代码的安全性和可维护性。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    public void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 清理代码
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // 清理代码
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, the `finalize` method should be defined as `protected`, rather than `public` or `private`. This ensures that only the class and its subclasses can call it.

##### Rule Meaning
Setting the `finalize` method to `protected` prevents unrelated classes from accessing it, thereby reducing potential errors and misuse. This aligns with the principle of encapsulation, enhancing the security and maintainability of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    public void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Cleanup code
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class MyClass {
    @Override
    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
        // Cleanup code
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
建议在遍历集合或数组时，使用增强for循环（foreach）而非传统for循环，以提高代码的可读性和简洁性。

##### 规则意义
使用增强for循环可以减少循环条件判断的复杂性，避免手动管理索引，从而减少出错的可能性，提高代码的清晰度和可维护性。

##### 错误示例
'''java
List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    System.out.println(list.get(i));
}
'''

##### 正确示例
'''java
List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
for (String element : list) {
    System.out.println(element);
}
'''

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
It is recommended to use the enhanced for loop (foreach) instead of the traditional for loop when traversing collections or arrays to improve the readability and conciseness of the code.

##### Rule Meaning
Using the enhanced for loop can reduce the complexity of loop condition checks, avoid manual index management, thereby decreasing the likelihood of errors, and improving the clarity and maintainability of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
'''java
List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    System.out.println(list.get(i));
}
'''

##### Correct Example
'''java
List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
for (String element : list) {
    System.out.println(element);
}
'''

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在某些情况下，使用 `while` 循环比 `for` 循环更符合逻辑，尤其是当循环条件需要在每次迭代中动态评估时。

##### 规则意义
`while` 循环能够提供更清晰的意图，尤其是在循环的结束条件不完全基于一个简单的计数器时。它也能够减少逻辑上的混淆，使代码更具可读性。使用 `while` 循环可以提高代码的可维护性。

##### 错误示例
```java
for (int i = 0; i < someValue; i++) {
    if (condition) {
        break;
    }
    // do something
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
int i = 0;
while (i < someValue) {
    if (condition) {
        break;
    }
    // do something
    i++;
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In certain situations, using a `while` loop is more logical than a `for` loop, especially when the loop condition needs to be evaluated dynamically in each iteration.

##### Rule Meaning
A `while` loop can provide clearer intent, particularly when the loop's termination condition is not entirely based on a simple counter. It can also reduce logical confusion, making the code more readable. Using a `while` loop can enhance the maintainability of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
for (int i = 0; i < someValue; i++) {
    if (condition) {
        break;
    }
    // do something
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
int i = 0;
while (i < someValue) {
    if (condition) {
        break;
    }
    // do something
    i++;
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在for循环中，变量的数量应尽可能少，避免引入不必要的复杂性。

##### 规则意义
多个循环变量可能会导致逻辑混乱，增加出错的风险，降低代码可读性和可维护性。使用过多的循环变量会使得代码难以理解和维护。建议单一循环变量或使用集合来处理多个元素。

##### 错误示例
```java
for (int i = 0, j = 0; i < 10 && j < 5; i++, j++) {
    System.out.println(i + " " + j);
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In for loops, the number of variables should be kept to a minimum to avoid introducing unnecessary complexity.

##### Rule Meaning
Multiple loop variables can lead to logical confusion, increase the risk of errors, and reduce code readability and maintainability. Using too many loop variables can make the code difficult to understand and maintain. It is recommended to use a single loop variable or to use collections to handle multiple elements.

##### Error Example
```java
for (int i = 0, j = 0; i < 10 && j < 5; i++, j++) {
    System.out.println(i + " " + j);
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
该规则要求Java方法的形式参数使用有意义且符合约定的命名方式。

##### 规则意义
采用一致的命名规范可增强代码的可读性和可维护性，使其他开发人员更容易理解方法的功能和参数的用途。

##### 错误示例
```java
public void calculateArea(int a, int b) {
    // 计算矩形面积
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public void calculateArea(int width, int height) {
    // 计算矩形面积
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule requires Java method formal parameters to use meaningful and convention-compliant naming.

##### Rule Meaning
Adopting consistent naming conventions can enhance code readability and maintainability, making it easier for other developers to understand the function of the method and the purpose of its parameters.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public void calculateArea(int a, int b) {
    // 计算矩形面积
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public void calculateArea(int width, int height) {
    // 计算矩形面积
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则要求在 Java 代码中使用适当的命名约定来命名泛型参数。

##### 规则意义
遵循一致的命名风格可以提高代码的可读性和可维护性，特别是在涉及泛型的情况下。通过使用明确的命名，开发者可以更容易地理解泛型的用途。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class Pair<K, V> {
    private K key;
    private V value;
}

public class Example {
    public void process(Pair<T, U> pair) { // 错误：泛型参数不符合命名约定
        // 处理逻辑
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class Pair<KeyType, ValueType> {
    private KeyType key;
    private ValueType value;
}

public class Example {
    public void process(Pair<K, V> pair) { // 正确：遵循命名约定
        // 处理逻辑
    }
}
```

