#### 规则概述
该规则指出在JUnit测试中，避免使用`@Test(expected = Exception.class)`来进行异常测试，推荐使用`try-catch`语句或`Assert.assertThrows()`方法来更清晰地控制异常的情况。

##### 规则意义
使用`@Test(expected = Exception.class)`可能导致错误的测试结果，特别是在多个异常被捕获的情况下，会隐藏真实的异常信息。而通过明确的异常处理可以提供更好的可读性和调试体验。

##### 错误示例
```java
@Test(expected = IOException.class)
public void testReadFile() {
    FileReader reader = new FileReader("nonexistent.txt");
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
@Test
public void testReadFile() {
    Exception exception = assertThrows(IOException.class, () -> {
        FileReader reader = new FileReader("nonexistent.txt");
    });
    assertEquals("File not found: nonexistent.txt", exception.getMessage());
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule indicates that in JUnit tests, it is advisable to avoid using `@Test(expected = Exception.class)` for exception testing. Instead, it is recommended to use `try-catch` statements or the `Assert.assertThrows()` method to better control exception scenarios more clearly.

##### Significance of the Rule
Using `@Test(expected = Exception.class)` can lead to incorrect test results, especially when multiple exceptions are caught, which can obscure the actual exception information. Explicit exception handling can provide better readability and debugging experience.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
@Test(expected = IOException.class)
public void testReadFile() {
    FileReader reader = new FileReader("nonexistent.txt");
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
@Test
public void testReadFile() {
    Exception exception = assertThrows(IOException.class, () -> {
        FileReader reader = new FileReader("nonexistent.txt");
    });
    assertEquals("File not found: nonexistent.txt", exception.getMessage());
}
```

