#### 规则概述
该规则检查远程接口（Remote Interface）是否按照命名约定进行命名。远程接口的命名应该以"Remote"作为前缀，以提高代码的可读性和维护性。

##### 规则意义
遵循命名约定可以提高代码的可理解性和一致性，尤其是在大型项目中，有助于开发人员快速识别远程接口。

##### 错误示例
'''java
public interface MyService {
    void performAction();
}
'''

##### 正确示例
'''java
public interface RemoteMyService {
    void performAction();
}
'''

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule checks whether remote interfaces are named according to naming conventions. Remote interfaces should be prefixed with "Remote" to enhance code readability and maintainability.

##### Rule Significance
Adhering to naming conventions can improve code comprehensibility and consistency, especially in large projects, helping developers quickly identify remote interfaces.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public interface MyService {
    void performAction();
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public interface RemoteMyService {
    void performAction();
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在Java中，`Enumeration`接口被许多类提供用于遍历集合，但它已经被`Iterator`接口所取代。使用`Iterator`可以提供更加灵活和安全的遍历方式，避免了潜在的错误。

##### 规则意义
使用`Iterator`替代`Enumeration`可以提高代码的可读性和可维护性，同时保证类型安全和简化代码逻辑。此外，使用`Iterator`还可以更好地支持并发修改问题，有助于避免在遍历集合时出现的异常。

##### 错误示例
```java
import java.util.Vector;

public class Example {
    public void iterateEnumeration() {
        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();
        Enumeration<String> enumeration = vector.elements();
        while (enumeration.hasMoreElements()) {
            String element = enumeration.nextElement();
            System.out.println(element);
        }
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;

public class Example {
    public void iterateIterator() {
        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        Iterator<String> iterator = list.iterator();
        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            String element = iterator.next();
            System.out.println(element);
        }
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, the `Enumeration` interface is provided by many classes for traversing collections, but it has been replaced by the `Iterator` interface. Using `Iterator` offers a more flexible and safe way to traverse collections, avoiding potential errors.

##### Rule Meaning
Replacing `Enumeration` with `Iterator` can enhance code readability and maintainability, while ensuring type safety and simplifying code logic. Additionally, using `Iterator` better supports issues related to concurrent modifications, helping to avoid exceptions that may occur while traversing a collection.

##### Error Example
```java
import java.util.Vector;

public class Example {
    public void iterateEnumeration() {
        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();
        Enumeration<String> enumeration = vector.elements();
        while (enumeration.hasMoreElements()) {
            String element = enumeration.nextElement();
            System.out.println(element);
        }
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;

public class Example {
    public void iterateIterator() {
        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        Iterator<String> iterator = list.iterator();
        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            String element = iterator.next();
            System.out.println(element);
        }
    }
}
```

