#### 规则概述
在Java中，`equals`方法用于比较对象的相等性。如果一个类的方法命名为`equals`但其实现并不符合逻辑，很容易导致程序运行时问题。该规则旨在捕获那些可能会引起混淆的以`equals`命名的方法。

##### 规则意义
名为`equals`的方法如果没有正确实现，会导致比较操作的误解，进而影响程序的逻辑和稳定性。遵循此规则可以减少代码中的潜在缺陷，提高代码的可读性和可维护性。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class User {
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        // 这里是错误的实现，可能导致与期望的equals逻辑不一致
        return false;
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class User {
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        User user = (User) obj;
        return Objects.equals(id, user.id) && Objects.equals(name, user.name);
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, the `equals` method is used to compare the equality of objects. If a class has a method named `equals` but its implementation does not conform to logic, it can easily lead to runtime issues. This rule aims to capture methods named `equals` that may cause confusion.

##### Rule Significance
A method named `equals` that is not implemented correctly can lead to misunderstandings in comparison operations, thereby affecting the program's logic and stability. Adhering to this rule can reduce potential defects in the code and improve readability and maintainability.

##### Error Example
```java
public class User {
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        // This is an incorrect implementation that may lead to inconsistencies with the expected equals logic
        return false;
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class User {
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        User user = (User) obj;
        return Objects.equals(id, user.id) && Objects.equals(name, user.name);
    }
}
```

