rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则用于检查 `switch` 语句的分支密度，确保代码可读性和可维护性。过多的分支可能导致代码复杂性增加，使得代码难以理解和维护。

##### 规则意义
过度使用 `switch` 语句可能会导致可读性差和可维护性差的问题。如果某个 `switch` 语句的分支数量过多，建议考虑其他代码结构，如使用多态来代替 `switch`。

##### 错误示例
```java
switch (value) {
    case 1:
        // 处理 1
        break;
    case 2:
        // 处理 2
        break;
    case 3:
        // 处理 3
        break;
    case 4:
        // 处理 4
        break;
    case 5:
        // 处理 5
        break;
    case 6:
        // 处理 6
        break;
    case 7:
        // 处理 7
        break;
    case 8:
        // 处理 8
        break;
    case 9:
        // 处理 9
        break;
    case 10:
        // 处理 10
        break;
    // 这里有太多的 case 分支，导致可读性降低
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
// 考虑使用多态代替过多的 switch 语句
abstract class Handler {
    abstract void handle();
}

class HandlerOne extends Handler {
    void handle() { /* 处理 1 */ }
}

class HandlerTwo extends Handler {
    void handle() { /* 处理 2 */ }
}

class Context {
    private Handler handler;

    public void setHandler(Handler handler) {
        this.handler = handler;
    }

    public void execute() {
        handler.handle();
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule is used to check the branch density of `switch` statements, ensuring code readability and maintainability. Excessive branches can lead to increased code complexity, making it difficult to understand and maintain the code.

##### Rule Meaning
Overusing `switch` statements may result in poor readability and maintainability. If the number of branches in a `switch` statement is too high, it is recommended to consider other code structures, such as using polymorphism instead of `switch`.

##### Error Example
```java
switch (value) {
    case 1:
        // Handle 1
        break;
    case 2:
        // Handle 2
        break;
    case 3:
        // Handle 3
        break;
    case 4:
        // Handle 4
        break;
    case 5:
        // Handle 5
        break;
    case 6:
        // Handle 6
        break;
    case 7:
        // Handle 7
        break;
    case 8:
        // Handle 8
        break;
    case 9:
        // Handle 9
        break;
    case 10:
        // Handle 10
        break;
    // Too many case branches here, resulting in decreased readability
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
// Consider using polymorphism instead of excessive switch statements
abstract class Handler {
    abstract void handle();
}

class HandlerOne extends Handler {
    void handle() { /* Handle 1 */ }
}

class HandlerTwo extends Handler {
    void handle() { /* Handle 2 */ }
}

class Context {
    private Handler handler;

    public void setHandler(Handler handler) {
        this.handler = handler;
    }

    public void execute() {
        handler.handle();
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在使用switch语句时，应该包含一个default分支，以处理未被列举的情况。这样可以确保即使有新的case添加到代码中，也能保持代码的健壮性和可维护性。

##### 规则意义
缺少default分支可能导致在switch语句中添加新的case后出现不可预期的行为，尤其是在未来的维护中。通过确保有一个default分支，可以更好地处理所有情况，从而提高代码的安全性和可靠性。

##### 错误示例
```java
public void example(int day) {
    switch (day) {
        case 1:
            System.out.println("Monday");
            break;
        case 2:
            System.out.println("Tuesday");
            break;
        // 缺少 default 分支
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public void example(int day) {
    switch (day) {
        case 1:
            System.out.println("Monday");
            break;
        case 2:
            System.out.println("Tuesday");
            break;
        default:
            System.out.println("Invalid day");
            break;
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
When using the switch statement, a default branch should be included to handle unlisted cases. This ensures that even if new cases are added to the code, the robustness and maintainability of the code can be preserved.

##### Rule Significance
The absence of a default branch may lead to unpredictable behavior after adding new cases to the switch statement, especially during future maintenance. By ensuring there is a default branch, all cases can be better handled, thus enhancing the safety and reliability of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public void example(int day) {
    switch (day) {
        case 1:
            System.out.println("Monday");
            break;
        case 2:
            System.out.println("Tuesday");
            break;
        // Missing default branch
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public void example(int day) {
    switch (day) {
        case 1:
            System.out.println("Monday");
            break;
        case 2:
            System.out.println("Tuesday");
            break;
        default:
            System.out.println("Invalid day");
            break;
    }
}
```

