rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在使用`switch`语句时，如果分支的数量过少，这可能会导致代码可读性差和可维护性低。通常，`switch`语句适用于需要处理多个条件的情况，而当分支数量不足时，可能更适合其他控制结构（如`if-else`）。

##### 规则意义
通过确保`switch`语句具有足够的分支，可以提高代码的可读性和可维护性，并且使得逻辑更清晰。

##### 错误示例
```java
int value = 1;

switch (value) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Value is one");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Value is two");
        break;
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
int value = 1;

if (value == 1) {
    System.out.println("Value is one");
} else if (value == 2) {
    System.out.println("Value is two");
} else {
    System.out.println("Value is something else");
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
When using the `switch` statement, having too few branches can lead to poor code readability and maintainability. Generally, `switch` statements are suitable for cases that involve handling multiple conditions, and when there are insufficient branches, other control structures (such as `if-else`) may be more appropriate.

##### Rule Meaning
By ensuring that a `switch` statement has enough branches, the readability and maintainability of the code can be improved, and the logic becomes clearer.

##### Error Example
```java
int value = 1;

switch (value) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Value is one");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Value is two");
        break;
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
int value = 1;

if (value == 1) {
    System.out.println("Value is one");
} else if (value == 2) {
    System.out.println("Value is two");
} else {
    System.out.println("Value is something else");
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则检测 `switch` 语句中分支的数量是否过少。通常，一个 `switch` 语句如果仅包含两个或更少的分支，可能不值得使用 `switch`，应考虑使用 `if` 语句替代。

##### 规则意义
使用 `switch` 语句的主要目的是提高代码的可读性和可维护性。较少的分支会导致代码不够简洁，反而增加理解的难度，因此应谨慎使用。

##### 错误示例
```java
public void example(int value) {
    switch (value) {
        case 1:
            System.out.println("One");
            break;
        case 2:
            System.out.println("Two");
            break;
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public void example(int value) {
    if (value == 1) {
        System.out.println("One");
    } else if (value == 2) {
        System.out.println("Two");
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule detects whether the number of branches in a `switch` statement is too few. Generally, if a `switch` statement contains only two or fewer branches, it may not be worth using `switch`, and it is advisable to consider using `if` statements instead.

##### Rule Meaning
The primary purpose of using a `switch` statement is to enhance the readability and maintainability of the code. Fewer branches can lead to code that is not concise enough, thereby increasing the difficulty of understanding it, so it should be used with caution.

##### Error Example
```java
public void example(int value) {
    switch (value) {
        case 1:
            System.out.println("One");
            break;
        case 2:
            System.out.println("Two");
            break;
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public void example(int value) {
    if (value == 1) {
        System.out.println("One");
    } else if (value == 2) {
        System.out.println("Two");
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
当类的字段数量过多时，会影响可读性、可维护性和可扩展性。

##### 规则意义
在面向对象编程中，一个类应该关注一个特定的责任。如果一个类包含过多的字段，可能意味着它承担了过多的职责，导致维护和理解变得更加困难。建议将功能相近的字段分组到一个新的类中，以提高代码的可读性和可管理性。

##### 错误示例
'''java
public class Example {
    private int field1;
    private int field2;
    private int field3;
    private int field4;
    private int field5;
    private int field6;
    private int field7;
    private int field8;
    private int field9;
    private int field10;
    private int field11;
    private int field12;
    private int field13;
    private int field14;
    private int field15;
}
'''

##### 正确示例
'''java
public class Example {
    private int id;
    private String name;
    private Address address;
}

class Address {
    private String street;
    private String city;
    private String zipCode;
}
'''

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
When a class has too many fields, it affects readability, maintainability, and scalability.

##### Rule Meaning
In object-oriented programming, a class should focus on a specific responsibility. If a class contains too many fields, it may indicate that it has taken on too many responsibilities, making maintenance and understanding more difficult. It is recommended to group closely related fields into a new class to improve the readability and manageability of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
'''java
public class Example {
    private int field1;
    private int field2;
    private int field3;
    private int field4;
    private int field5;
    private int field6;
    private int field7;
    private int field8;
    private int field9;
    private int field10;
    private int field11;
    private int field12;
    private int field13;
    private int field14;
    private int field15;
}
'''

##### Correct Example
'''java
public class Example {
    private int id;
    private String name;
    private Address address;
}

class Address {
    private String street;
    private String city;
    private String zipCode;
}
'''

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
当一个类的方法数目过多时，可能导致代码可读性差、维护困难和理解复杂性增加，因此应限制类中的方法数量。

##### 规则意义
遵循此规则可以提高代码的可维护性和可读性，降低代码耦合度，从而减少潜在错误，并改善代码整体设计。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class Example {
    public void method1() {}
    public void method2() {}
    public void method3() {}
    public void method4() {}
    public void method5() {}
    public void method6() {}
    public void method7() {}
    public void method8() {}
    public void method9() {}
    public void method10() {}
    public void method11() {}
    public void method12() {}
    public void method13() {}
    public void method14() {}
    public void method15() {}
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class Example {
    public void method1() {}
    public void method2() {}
}

public class ExampleHelper {
    public void method3() {}
    public void method4() {}
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
When a class has too many methods, it can lead to poor readability, difficult maintenance, and increased complexity in understanding, so the number of methods in a class should be limited.

##### Rule Meaning
Adhering to this rule can improve the maintainability and readability of the code, reduce coupling, thereby minimizing potential errors and enhancing the overall design of the code.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
public class Example {
    public void method1() {}
    public void method2() {}
    public void method3() {}
    public void method4() {}
    public void method5() {}
    public void method6() {}
    public void method7() {}
    public void method8() {}
    public void method9() {}
    public void method10() {}
    public void method11() {}
    public void method12() {}
    public void method13() {}
    public void method14() {}
    public void method15() {}
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class Example {
    public void method1() {}
    public void method2() {}
}

public class ExampleHelper {
    public void method3() {}
    public void method4() {}
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则用于检测一个类中使用了过多的静态导入。过多的静态导入可能会导致代码可读性下降，增加混淆风险。

##### 规则意义
使用静态导入可以使代码更简洁，但如果静态导入数量过多，可能会导致命名冲突和不清晰的上下文，进而影响代码维护性。

##### 错误示例
```java
import static java.lang.Math.*;
import static java.util.Arrays.*;

public class Example {
    public void example() {
        System.out.println(random());
        print(new int[]{1, 2, 3});
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import static java.lang.Math.random;
import static java.util.Arrays.print;

public class Example {
    public void example() {
        System.out.println(random());
        print(new int[]{1, 2, 3});
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule is used to detect excessive use of static imports in a class. Too many static imports may lead to decreased code readability and increased risk of confusion.

##### Rule Significance
Using static imports can make the code more concise, but if the number of static imports is too high, it may cause naming conflicts and unclear context, thus affecting code maintainability.

##### Error Example
```java
import static java.lang.Math.*;
import static java.util.Arrays.*;

public class Example {
    public void example() {
        System.out.println(random());
        print(new int[]{1, 2, 3});
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import static java.lang.Math.random;
import static java.util.Arrays.print;

public class Example {
    public void example() {
        System.out.println(random());
        print(new int[]{1, 2, 3});
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
该规则检测类中未注释的空构造函数。这意味着构造函数的实现为空，并且没有提供任何注释来解释其存在的原因。

##### 规则意义
未注释的空构造函数可能导致代码可读性下降，其他开发人员在维护代码时无法理解构造函数的目的。提供详细的注释可以帮助其他开发人员更好地理解类的设计思路。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class Example {
    public Example() {
        // 空构造函数
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
public class Example {
    /**
     * 默认构造函数，用于创建Example类的实例。
     */
    public Example() {
        // 空构造函数
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule detects unannotated empty constructors in classes. This means the implementation of the constructor is empty and no comments are provided to explain the reason for its existence.

##### Rule Meaning
Unannotated empty constructors can lead to decreased code readability, making it difficult for other developers to understand the purpose of the constructor when maintaining the code. Providing detailed comments can help other developers better understand the design intent of the class.

##### Error Example
```java
public class Example {
    public Example() {
        // Empty constructor
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
public class Example {
    /**
     * Default constructor for creating an instance of the Example class.
     */
    public Example() {
        // Empty constructor
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则检测未注释的空方法体，以确保代码可读性和可维护性。

##### 规则意义
未注释的空方法体可能导致代码混乱，使得其他开发者难以理解该方法的用途及其逻辑。注释能够提供必要的信息，帮助团队成员有效地使用和维护代码。

##### 错误示例
```java
public void myMethod() {
    // 方法体为空
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
/**
 * 该方法用于执行特定的操作。
 */
public void myMethod() {
    // TODO: Implement method logic
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule detects un-commented empty method bodies to ensure code readability and maintainability.

##### Rule Meaning
Un-commented empty method bodies can lead to code confusion, making it difficult for other developers to understand the purpose and logic of the method. Comments provide necessary information to help team members effectively use and maintain the code.

##### Error Example
```java
public void myMethod() {
    // 方法体为空
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
/**
 * This method is used to perform a specific operation.
 */
public void myMethod() {
    // TODO: Implement method logic
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则用于检测不必要的条件语句，例如始终为真或始终为假的条件语句，可能导致不必要的代码执行。

##### 规则意义
不必要的条件语句会增加代码复杂性，降低代码的可读性和可维护性。它们往往是逻辑错误的体现，可能会导致意想不到的行为或无法预见的bug。

##### 错误示例
```java
if (true) {
    System.out.println("This will always execute.");
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
// 移除不必要的条件语句
System.out.println("This will always execute.");
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
This rule is used to detect unnecessary conditional statements, such as conditions that are always true or always false, which may lead to unnecessary code execution.

##### Rule Significance
Unnecessary conditional statements increase code complexity and reduce code readability and maintainability. They often represent logical errors and can lead to unexpected behavior or unforeseen bugs.

##### Error Example
```java
if (true) {
    System.out.println("This will always execute.");
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
// Remove unnecessary conditional statements
System.out.println("This will always execute.");
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在单元测试中，如果断言失败，提供有意义的错误消息可以帮助开发人员更快地定位问题。

##### 规则意义
当单元测试中的断言出现失败时，缺少详细的错误消息会导致调试变得困难。使用自定义消息，使得测试的维护和调试变得更为高效。

##### 错误示例
```java
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;

public class ExampleTest {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testAddition() {
        assertEquals(3, 1 + 1);
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;

public class ExampleTest {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testAddition() {
        assertEquals("1 + 1 should equal 2", 2, 1 + 1);
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In unit testing, providing meaningful error messages when assertions fail can help developers locate problems more quickly.

##### Significance of the Rule
When assertions fail in unit tests, the lack of detailed error messages can make debugging difficult. Using custom messages makes maintenance and debugging of tests more efficient.

##### Error Example
```java
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;

public class ExampleTest {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testAddition() {
        assertEquals(3, 1 + 1);
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;

public class ExampleTest {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testAddition() {
        assertEquals("1 + 1 should equal 2", 2, 1 + 1);
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
单元测试中包含过多的断言会导致测试难以维护和理解，增加了测试失败时的排查难度。建议每个测试方法只包含一种行为或功能，避免过多的断言。

##### 规则意义
遵循单一责任原则，每个单元测试应专注于一个特定的功能或行为，这样可以更清晰地识别错误并提高测试的可读性和可维护性。

##### 错误示例
```java
@Test
public void testComplexMethod() {
    assertEquals(expectedValue1, methodUnderTest());
    assertTrue(condition1);
    assertFalse(condition2);
    assertEquals(expectedValue2, anotherMethod());
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
@Test
public void testMethodWithExpectedValue() {
    assertEquals(expectedValue, methodUnderTest());
}

@Test
public void testMethodWithCondition1() {
    assertTrue(condition1);
}

@Test
public void testMethodWithCondition2() {
    assertFalse(condition2);
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
Having too many assertions in a unit test makes it difficult to maintain and understand, increasing the difficulty of troubleshooting when tests fail. It is recommended that each test method contains only one behavior or functionality to avoid excessive assertions.

##### Rule Significance
By adhering to the Single Responsibility Principle, each unit test should focus on a specific functionality or behavior, allowing for clearer identification of errors and enhancing the readability and maintainability of the tests.

##### Error Example
```java
@Test
public void testComplexMethod() {
    assertEquals(expectedValue1, methodUnderTest());
    assertTrue(condition1);
    assertFalse(condition2);
    assertEquals(expectedValue2, anotherMethod());
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
@Test
public void testMethodWithExpectedValue() {
    assertEquals(expectedValue, methodUnderTest());
}

@Test
public void testMethodWithCondition1() {
    assertTrue(condition1);
}

@Test
public void testMethodWithCondition2() {
    assertFalse(condition2);
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
单元测试的目的是验证代码的正确性。如果一个单元测试没有包含断言，它将不会验证任何行为，因此失去了测试的意义。

##### 规则意义
确保每个单元测试都包含至少一个断言，以验证目标代码的实际行为是否符合预期。这有助于发现潜在的错误和缺陷。

##### 错误示例
'''java
import org.junit.Test;

public class SampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testAddition() {
        int result = 1 + 1;
        // 缺少断言，不能验证结果
    }
}
'''

##### 正确示例
'''java
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.*;

public class SampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testAddition() {
        int result = 1 + 1;
        assertEquals(2, result); // 断言验证结果
    }
}
'''

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
The purpose of unit testing is to validate the correctness of the code. If a unit test does not contain an assertion, it will not verify any behavior, thus losing the significance of the test.

##### Rule Significance
Ensure that each unit test contains at least one assertion to verify whether the actual behavior of the target code meets expectations. This helps to uncover potential errors and defects.

##### Incorrect Example
'''java
import org.junit.Test;

public class SampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testAddition() {
        int result = 1 + 1;
        // Missing assertion, cannot verify the result
    }
}
'''

##### Correct Example
'''java
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.*;

public class SampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testAddition() {
        int result = 1 + 1;
        assertEquals(2, result); // Assertion verifies the result
    }
}
'''

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在编写单元测试时，若测试方法之间有需要清理或重置的资源，应使用 @After 注解来确保每次测试执行结束后都能进行必要的清理工作。

##### 规则意义
使用 @After 注解可以确保每个测试的执行环境是独立的，从而避免测试之间的相互影响，提高测试的稳定性和可靠性。

##### 错误示例
```java
import org.junit.Test;

public class ExampleTest {
    private int counter = 0;

    @Test
    public void testIncrement() {
        counter++;
    }

    @Test
    public void testReset() {
        counter = 0; // 没有使用 @After 清理，可能导致测试不可靠
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import org.junit.After;
import org.junit.Test;

public class ExampleTest {
    private int counter = 0;

    @After
    public void resetCounter() {
        counter = 0; // 使用 @After 注解进行清理
    }

    @Test
    public void testIncrement() {
        counter++;
    }

    @Test
    public void testReset() {
        // 每次测试前 counter 会被重置
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
When writing unit tests, if there are resources that need to be cleaned up or reset between test methods, the @After annotation should be used to ensure that necessary cleanup work is performed after each test execution.

##### Rule Meaning
Using the @After annotation ensures that the execution environment for each test is independent, thereby avoiding interference between tests and improving the stability and reliability of the tests.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
import org.junit.Test;

public class ExampleTest {
    private int counter = 0;

    @Test
    public void testIncrement() {
        counter++;
    }

    @Test
    public void testReset() {
        counter = 0; // No cleanup using @After, which may lead to unreliable tests
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import org.junit.After;
import org.junit.Test;

public class ExampleTest {
    private int counter = 0;

    @After
    public void resetCounter() {
        counter = 0; // Cleanup using @After annotation
    }

    @Test
    public void testIncrement() {
        counter++;
    }

    @Test
    public void testReset() {
        // The counter will be reset before each test
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
单元测试类中的重复代码应该用@Before注解进行重构，以提高代码的可维护性和可读性。

##### 规则意义
使用@Before注解可以避免在每个测试方法中重复设置相同的初始化代码，简化测试代码，提高测试执行效率。

##### 错误示例
```java
import org.junit.Test;

public class SampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testMethod1() {
        // 初始化代码
        Database db = new Database();
        db.connect();
        // 测试逻辑
    }

    @Test
    public void testMethod2() {
        // 初始化代码
        Database db = new Database();
        db.connect();
        // 测试逻辑
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import org.junit.Before;
import org.junit.Test;

public class SampleTest {
    private Database db;

    @Before
    public void setUp() {
        // 初始化代码
        db = new Database();
        db.connect();
    }

    @Test
    public void testMethod1() {
        // 测试逻辑
    }

    @Test
    public void testMethod2() {
        // 测试逻辑
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
Duplicate code in unit test classes should be refactored using the @Before annotation to improve code maintainability and readability.

##### Rule Meaning
Using the @Before annotation can prevent repetitive setup of the same initialization code in each test method, simplifying the test code and increasing test execution efficiency.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
import org.junit.Test;

public class SampleTest {
    @Test
    public void testMethod1() {
        // 初始化代码
        Database db = new Database();
        db.connect();
        // 测试逻辑
    }

    @Test
    public void testMethod2() {
        // 初始化代码
        Database db = new Database();
        db.connect();
        // 测试逻辑
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import org.junit.Before;
import org.junit.Test;

public class SampleTest {
    private Database db;

    @Before
    public void setUp() {
        // 初始化代码
        db = new Database();
        db.connect();
    }

    @Test
    public void testMethod1() {
        // 测试逻辑
    }

    @Test
    public void testMethod2() {
        // 测试逻辑
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在Java中，单元测试方法应使用@Test注解来标识，以便能够被JUnit测试框架正确执行。

##### 规则意义
使用@Test注解可以确保测试方法被JUnit识别并正确运行。未使用注解的方法将不会执行，可能导致未测试的代码未能被发现，这会导致潜在的缺陷未被及时发现。

##### 错误示例
```java
public class MyTests {
    public void testMethod() {
        // 这是一个测试方法，但缺少@Test注解
        assertEquals(1, 1);
    }
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;

public class MyTests {
    @Test
    public void testMethod() {
        // 测试方法使用@Test注解
        assertEquals(1, 1);
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java, unit test methods should be marked with the @Test annotation to be correctly executed by the JUnit testing framework.

##### Rule Importance
Using the @Test annotation ensures that the test methods are recognized and executed correctly by JUnit. Methods that do not use the annotation will not be executed, potentially leading to untested code going undetected, which could result in latent defects not being identified in a timely manner.

##### Wrong Example
```java
public class MyTests {
    public void testMethod() {
        // This is a test method but missing the @Test annotation
        assertEquals(1, 1);
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;

public class MyTests {
    @Test
    public void testMethod() {
        // Test method uses the @Test annotation
        assertEquals(1, 1);
    }
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
在Java代码中，某些注解的值元素可能是多余的，尤其是在默认值已被使用的情况下。使用不必要的值会使代码变得冗余，降低可读性。

##### 规则意义
避免在注释中使用不必要的值元素可以提高代码的简洁性和可读性，同时减少潜在的误解和维护成本。

##### 错误示例
```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@interface Example {
    String value() default "defaultValue";
}

@Example(value = "defaultValue") // 不必要的值元素
public class UnnecessaryAnnotation {
}
```

##### 正确示例
```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@interface Example {
    String value() default "defaultValue";
}

@Example // 省略不必要的值元素
public class NecessaryAnnotation {
}
```

rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
In Java code, certain annotation value elements may be redundant, especially when default values are already in use. Using unnecessary values makes the code verbose and reduces readability.

##### Significance of the Rule
Avoiding the use of unnecessary value elements in annotations can enhance the conciseness and readability of the code, while also reducing potential misunderstandings and maintenance costs.

##### Incorrect Example
```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@interface Example {
    String value() default "defaultValue";
}

@Example(value = "defaultValue") // unnecessary value element
public class UnnecessaryAnnotation {
}
```

##### Correct Example
```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@interface Example {
    String value() default "defaultValue";
}

@Example // omit unnecessary value element
public class NecessaryAnnotation {
}
```

rules.content:
#### 规则概述
此规则检测代码中是否存在不必要的布尔值断言。布尔值断言通常用来验证条件的真或假，但在某些情况下是多余的。

##### 规则意义
不必要的布尔断言会导致代码的可读性降低，并可能引发误解。清晰和简洁的代码有助于提高可维护性。

##### 错误示例
```java
if (booleanCondition == true) {
    doSomething();
}
```

##### 正确示例
